            <ArticleTitle>Olfactory Groove Meningiomas: Acute Presentation and Potential : Pitfalls in Management and Functional Restoration.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>155-158</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/978-3-319-39546-3_24</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Meningiomas are typically benign, slow-growing lesions that present after an insidious onset of symptoms related to mass effect. The acute presentation of a patient who has suffered a transtentorial herniation event due to a meningioma is rare. There are only few publications describing such a presentation in the absence of hemorrhage [1]. In this case report, a patient with an olfactory groove meningioma presenting with signs and symptoms of transtentorial herniation in the absence of tumor-associated hemorrhage is discussed. This is a unique presentation of such a lesion. The patient developed Anton's syndrome-binocular visual loss with blindness denial. Management considerations for patients with meningiomas that present with acute deterioration are discussed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
